{
  "title": "Where is it?",
  "title_target": "אֵיפֹה זֶה?",
  "goal": "Ask for and give directions, name places in town — and cross this module's most important bridge: reading Hebrew without niqqud, as on real signs.",
  "bekal": [
    "asking the way and understanding the answer",
    "saying where things are with prepositions",
    "reading Hebrew without niqqud"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "שִׂיחָה — מְחַפְּשִׂים אֶת הַתַּחֲנָה",
      "intro": "Budi is lost in downtown Jerusalem and asks a woman passing by.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "סְלִיחָה, אֵיפֹה תַּחֲנַת הָאוֹטוֹבּוּס?",
          "baca": "slikhah, eifoh takhanat ha'otobus?",
          "id": "Excuse me, where's the bus stop?",
          "literal": "sorry, where the-stop the-bus?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "אִשָּׁה:",
          "text": "לֵךְ יָשָׁר עַד הָרַמְזוֹר.",
          "baca": "lekh yashar ad haramzor.",
          "id": "Go straight to the traffic light.",
          "literal": "go straight until the-traffic-light."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "וְאָז?",
          "baca": "ve'az?",
          "id": "And then?",
          "literal": "and-then?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "אִשָּׁה:",
          "text": "אָז פְּנֵה שְׂמֹאלָה, לִרְחוֹב יָפוֹ.",
          "baca": "az pneh smolah, lirkhov yafo.",
          "id": "Then turn left, onto Jaffa Street.",
          "literal": "then turn to-left, to-street yafo."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "זֶה רָחוֹק?",
          "baca": "zeh rakhok?",
          "id": "Is it far?",
          "literal": "this far?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "אִשָּׁה:",
          "text": "לֹא, חָמֵשׁ דַּקּוֹת בְּרֶגֶל.",
          "baca": "lo, khamesh dakot beregel.",
          "id": "No, five minutes on foot.",
          "literal": "no, five minutes with-foot."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "הַתַּחֲנָה מוּל הַבַּנְק?",
          "baca": "hatakhanah mul habank?",
          "id": "Is the stop opposite the bank?",
          "literal": "the-stop opposite the-bank?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "אִשָּׁה:",
          "text": "לֹא, לְיַד הַדֹּאַר, אַחֲרֵי הָרְחוֹב.",
          "baca": "lo, leyad hado'ar, akharei harkhov.",
          "id": "No, next to the post office, past the street.",
          "literal": "no, next-to the-post, after the-street."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "וְאֵיזֶה אוֹטוֹבּוּס נוֹסֵעַ לַתַּחֲנָה הַמֶּרְכָּזִית?",
          "baca": "ve'eizeh otobus nose'a latakhanah hamerkazit?",
          "id": "And which bus goes to the central station?",
          "literal": "and-which bus goes to-the-station the-central?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "אִשָּׁה:",
          "text": "מִסְפָּר שְׁמוֹנֶה עֶשְׂרֵה אוֹ עֶשְׂרִים וְאַחַת.",
          "baca": "mispar shmoneh esreh o esrim ve'akhat.",
          "id": "Number eighteen or twenty-one.",
          "literal": "number eighteen or twenty and-one."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "בּוּדִי:",
          "text": "תּוֹדָה רַבָּה! עָזַרְתְּ לִי מְאוֹד.",
          "baca": "todah rabah! azart li me'od.",
          "id": "Thanks so much! You've been a great help.",
          "literal": "thanks much! you-helped (f) to-me very."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "אִשָּׁה:",
          "text": "בְּכֵיף. שֶׁיִּהְיֶה לְךָ יוֹם טוֹב!",
          "baca": "bekeif. sheyihyeh lekha yom tov!",
          "id": "My pleasure. Have a nice day!",
          "literal": "with-fun. may-be to-you day good!"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**לֵךְ יָשָׁר** and **פְּנֵה שְׂמֹאלָה** are **imperatives**. To a woman the form changes: לְכִי, פְּנִי. Full imperatives come in unit 19; for now just recognize the forms you **hear**, since you'll receive directions more often than give them.",
        "**The directional ending ־ָה is back**: שְׂמֹאלָה (to the left), יָמִינָה (to the right), יָשָׁר (straight, no ending). Just like הַבַּיְתָה in unit 10.",
        "**Place prepositions stand alone — they don't attach**: לְיַד (next to), מוּל (opposite), בֵּין (between), מֵאֲחוֹרֵי (behind), לִפְנֵי (in front of). Only בְּ (in) attaches; עַל (on) is its own word too.",
        "⚠️ **לִפְנֵי means both \"before\" AND \"in front of\"** — the sentence tells you which. לִפְנֵי הַשִּׁעוּר = before the lesson; לִפְנֵי הַבַּיִת = in front of the house. Hebrew uses one spatial image for time and place, much as English says \"the days ahead\".",
        "**בְּרֶגֶל** (\"by foot\") = on foot. Related: בְּאוֹטוֹבּוּס (by bus), בְּמוֹנִית (by taxi), בְּרַכֶּבֶת (by train). All means of transport take בְּ־, like English \"by\".",
        "**עָזַרְתְּ** in line 11 is **past tense** — the first past-tense verb you've met. Unit 15 takes it apart; for now just know that the ending ־ְתְּ marks \"you (f) did\".",
        "**בְּכֵיף** (*bekeif*, \"with pleasure\") is a casual reply to thanks. The more standard one: **בְּבַקָּשָׁה** — the same word for \"please\" and \"you're welcome\", just like German *bitte*.",
        "**הַתַּחֲנָה הַמֶּרְכָּזִית** = the central bus station. Note הַ־ twice: definite noun + definite adjective, the rule from unit 6."
      ],
